\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The sign of a lift on a representation}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative ring in which #{2} is invertible.
Let #{V} be a #{k}-module carrying a representation of the group #{\operatorname{Pin}(B_\Lambda;\Gamma)}
in \ref{fdip-016U}. The action of its central element
#{-\bbI_S} splits #{V} into two invariant eigenspaces of signs
#{+1,-1}. The representation factors through #{B_\Lambda}
exactly when the negative space is zero. Tensor products of
sign spaces have the product sign. On #{S} itself the sign
is negative.}

\p{For any available equal-period quarter-turn, #{u_{jk}} has
order #{8}. If #{k} is a field containing a primitive eighth root #{\zeta},
the degree #{\nu} for its \vocabk{cyclic grading}{fdip-016I} has
central sign #{(-1)^\nu}. This assertion uses the cyclic subgroup
generated by #{u_{jk}}, including when its coordinate image
belongs to a larger momentum stabilizer.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{z} for the action of #{-\bbI_S} on #{V}.
Since #{z^2=\bbI_V}, the operators #{(\bbI_V\pm z)/2} are
complementary projections to its sign spaces. Centrality
makes both spaces invariant. If #{z} acts as identity, the two
lifts of each coordinate change act alike by \ref{fdip-016U}.
Their common action is a representation downstairs. Conversely
an action through the coordinate group kills the kernel.
On a pure tensor the action of #{z} multiplies the two scalar
signs, and #{-\bbI_S} negates every vector of #{S}.}

\p{For #{b=\varepsilon_j\varepsilon_k}, the equality #{b^2=-\bbI_S}
gives #{u_{jk}^2=b}, #{u_{jk}^4=-\bbI_S}, and
#{u_{jk}^8=\bbI_S}. The fourth power differs from the identity
on the nonzero complex carrier, proving order #{8} in the group.
In the given representation #{z} acts as the fourth power of
the represented quarter-turn. The finite cyclic projections of
\ref{fdip-016K} split the degrees, and on degree #{\nu} that
fourth power is #{\zeta^{4\nu}=(-1)^\nu}.}
}
